WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.400
Two last points on distress.

2
00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:11.550
The Inland rules add one more signal: a high-intensity white light flashing at regular intervals, fifty to seventy times a minute.

3
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:17.475
Second, these signals are for one purpose only: to show distress and the need for help.

4
00:00:17.725 --> 00:00:20.875
Using them for anything else is prohibited.

5
00:00:21.125 --> 00:00:25.075
So is using any signal that might be confused with them.

6
00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:28.275
Which brings us to a favorite exam question.

7
00:00:28.525 --> 00:00:32.900
It lists four signals and asks which one is not a distress signal.

8
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:34.725
So try it.

9
00:00:34.975 --> 00:00:41.150
Orange smoke, a continuously sounding fog horn, three short blasts, or a red hand flare.

10
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.475
Which one doesn’t belong?

11
00:00:44.475 --> 00:00:46.425
Three short blasts.

12
00:00:46.675 --> 00:00:48.825
That’s astern propulsion.

13
00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:52.225
Watch for a green flare or a white flare too.

14
00:00:52.475 --> 00:00:54.725
Neither is a distress signal.

15
00:00:54.975 --> 00:00:57.025
That’s lesson fourteen.

16
00:00:57.275 --> 00:01:04.175
In fog: one prolonged for power making way, two for stopped, one and two short for the hampered vessels.

17
00:01:04.425 --> 00:01:06.500
A rapid bell at anchor.

18
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.925
And for distress, think red, orange, continuous, Mayday.

19
00:01:11.175 --> 00:01:17.575
Next time, our last Rules lesson, we pull together every Inland and International difference.
